3. Two Implementation Methods
Solution A – Exact Algorithm
- Checks different combinations of candidate centres.
- Finds the minimum number of centres required.
- Guarantees an optimal solution if a solution exists.
- Requires more computational effort for larger problems.
Solution B – Greedy Approximation Algorithm
- Counts how many uncovered zones each centre can cover.
- Selects the centre covering the maximum number of uncovered zones.
- Marks those zones as covered.
- Repeats until all zones are covered.
- Usually requires less computational effort, but does not always give the minimum solution.
4. Python Source Code
The following is the complete implementation for both algorithms, including performance counters, test cases, a counterexample and scalability experiments.
from itertools import combinations# ----------------------------------------# SOLUTION A: EXACT SET COVER# ----------------------------------------def exact_set_cover(zones, centers):    center_names = list(centers.keys())    zone_set = set(zones)    search_states = 0    # Try combinations in increasing size    for size in range(len(center_names) + 1):        for selected in combinations(center_names, size):            search_states += 1            covered = set()            for center in selected:                covered.update(centers[center])            # Check whether all zones are covered            if zone_set.issubset(covered):                return list(selected), search_states    return None, search_states# ----------------------------------------



