8. B3.4 – Engineering Trade-Off (2 Marks)
Comparison of Exact and Greedy Algorithms
Feature	Exact Algorithm	Greedy Algorithm
Solution quality	Always optimal	May not be optimal
Search effort	High	Low
Execution	Slower for large inputs	Usually faster
Backtracking	Not required in our combination-based implementation	Not required
Scalability	Limited for large instances	Better for large instances
Guarantee	Minimum cover	No general optimality guarantee

What did the approximate solution gain?
1. Reduced computational effort.
2. Faster selection of emergency service centres.
3. Fewer combinations to examine.
4. Better practical performance for larger datasets.
What did it sacrifice?
1. It does not guarantee the minimum number of centres.
2. It may select unnecessary centres.
3. Its result depends on the order and coverage of available centres.
Realistic Situation
Consider an emergency situation such as a flood or forest fire.

The government needs to arrange ambulance stations and emergency response centres across several affected zones.

In this situation, finding a quick coverage solution is more important than spending a long time calculating the mathematically perfect minimum.

Therefore, the greedy algorithm is acceptable because it can quickly provide a complete coverage plan.

However, for long-term emergency infrastructure planning, the exact algorithm is preferable when the number of zones and candidate centres is small enough.
